  "model": "claude-4.6-opus-high-thinking",
  "text": "Here is a comprehensive overview of the Grafana codebase and its React Router architecture and migration.\n\n---\n\n## Codebase Overview\n\nGrafana is a large **monorepo** with a Go backend and a React/TypeScript frontend.\n\n### Top-Level Structure\n\n| Directory | Purpose |\n|-----------|---------|\n| `public/app/` | Frontend application source (React/TypeScript) |\n| `pkg/` | Go backend (API handlers, services, models, storage) |\n| `packages/` | Shared npm packages (`@grafana/ui`, `@grafana/data`, `@grafana/runtime`, etc.) |\n| `apps/` | SDK-style Grafana apps (advisor, alerting, dashboard, plugins, etc.) |\n| `conf/` | Configuration files |\n| `scripts/` | Build and tooling scripts |\n| `e2e/`, `e2e-playwright/` | End-to-end tests |\n| `devenv/` | Development environment setup |\n\n### Tech Stack\n\n- **Frontend:** React 18, Redux Toolkit, Webpack 5, Emotion (CSS-in-JS), Nx for orchestration\n- **Backend:** Go 1.25, Chi (HTTP routing), GORM (SQL), Wire (dependency injection), Kubernetes-style API server\n- **Routing:** React Router v5 with a v5-compat bridge to v6 APIs (`react-router-dom-v5-compat`)\n\n---\n\n## Frontend Application Architecture\n\n### Bootstrap Flow\n\n```\nindex.ts  →  waits for window.grafanaBootData\n  └── initApp.ts  →  loads trusted type policies\n        └── app.ts (GrafanaApp.init())\n              ├── Initializes i18n, services, plugins, Redux store\n              └── Renders <AppWrapper /> into #reactRoot\n```\n\n### Component Hierarchy\n\n```\nAppWrapper\n  ├── Redux Provider, GrafanaContext, ThemeProvider, KBarProvider\n  └── RouterWrapper (public/app/routes/RoutesWrapper.tsx)\n        ├── Router (history = locationService.getHistory())\n        ├── LocationServiceProvider\n        ├── CompatRouter (react-router-dom-v5-compat bridge)\n        ├── AppChrome (shell / layout)\n        └── <Routes>\n              └── <Route> per RouteDescriptor\n                    └── GrafanaRouteWrapper\n                          ├── Auth checks (redirect to /login if unauthenticated)\n                          ├── RBAC role checks\n                          └── GrafanaRoute\n                                ├── ErrorBoundary + Suspense\n                                └── route.component({ route, location, queryParams })\n```\n\n### Route Definitions\n\nAll routes are defined centrally in `public/app/routes/routes.tsx` via `getAppRoutes()`, which returns an array of `RouteDescriptor` objects:\n\n```typescript\ninterface RouteDescriptor {\n  path: string;\n  component: GrafanaRouteComponent;\n  roles?: () => string[];\n  chromeless?: boolean;\n  sensitive?: boolean;\n  allowAnonymous?: boolean | ((params) => boolean);\n}\n```\n\nRoutes are lazily loaded using `SafeDynamicImport` (a thin wrapper around `React.lazy`). Feature-specific routes are aggregated from separate modules (alerting, provisioning, dashboard-scene, plugins, etc.).\n\n---\n\n## React Router Architecture\n\n### Current State: Hybrid v5/v6\n\nGrafana currently runs **React Router v5** as the actual router, but uses a **v5-compat bridge** (`react-router-dom-v5-compat`) that exposes React Router v6 APIs. This means:\n\n- The `Router` component uses `locationService.getHistory()` (a v5-style `history` object)\n- `CompatRouter` wraps the tree so v6 hooks (`useNavigate`, `useLocation`, `useParams`) work within the v5 router\n- Route rendering uses v6's `<Routes>` and `<Route>` components from the compat package\n\n### `locationService` — The Legacy Navigation Singleton\n\nDefined in `packages/grafana-runtime/src/services/LocationService.tsx`, this is a singleton wrapper around the `history` library:\n\n```typescript\nimport { locationService } from '@grafana/runtime';\n\nlocationService.push('/path');              // Navigate to path\nlocationService.replace('/path');           // Replace current history entry\nlocationService.partial({ key: 'value' }); // Update query params only\nlocationService.getHistory();               // Get the history object (used by Router)\nlocationService.getLocation();              // Get current location\n```\n\nIt also powers:\n- **Link interception** (`interceptLinkClicks.ts`) — a document-level click handler that intercepts `<a>` clicks and routes them through `locationService.push`\n- **Query param hooks** like `useUrlParams()` and `useQueryParams()` — both backed by `locationService.partial`\n\n### `useNavigate` — The New Pattern\n\nThe migration target is React Router's `useNavigate` hook, imported from the compat package:\n\n```typescript\nimport { useNavigate } from 'react-router-dom-v5-compat';\n\nconst navigate = useNavigate();\nnavigate('/path');\nnavigate('/path', { replace: true });\n```\n\n---\n\n## The Migration\n\n### What's Being Migrated\n\nThe migration replaces **all uses of `locationService.push/replace/partial`** with React Router's `useNavigate` hook (and related hooks). This moves navigation from an imperative singleton pattern to React's component-driven paradigm.\n\n### Migration Strategy\n\nThe migration is **incremental and feature-flagged**. Each area of the app can be independently switched over:\n\n1. **Feature flags** are defined in `pkg/services/featuremgmt/` (e.g., `playlistUseNavigate`, `teamsUseNavigate`, `serviceAccountsUseNavigate`)\n2. Components are updated to conditionally use the new pattern:\n\n```typescript\nif (config.featureToggles.playlistUseNavigate) {\n  navigate('/path');\n} else {\n  locationService.push('/path');\n}\n```\n\n3. Once validated, the feature flag is removed and the old code is deleted\n\n### Migration Progress\n\n| Metric | Count |\n|--------|-------|\n| Files using `locationService.push` | ~80 |\n| Files using `locationService.replace` | ~24 |\n| Files using `locationService.partial` | ~50 |\n| Files using `useNavigate` | ~85 |\n\n**Estimated completion:** roughly 40% done.\n\n- **Fully migrated:** Provisioning pages use `useNavigate` directly with no feature flag\n- **Feature flags defined but not yet wired:** Playlist, Teams, Service Accounts\n- **Not yet started:** Many core areas (alerting, explore, dashboard settings, data sources, etc.)\n\n### Infrastructure Still Pending\n\nEven beyond individual page migrations, several foundational pieces still use `locationService`:\n\n| File | Concern |\n|------|---------|\n| `public/app/core/navigation/hooks.ts` | `useUrlParams()` uses `locationService.partial` |\n| `public/app/core/hooks/useQueryParams.ts` | `useQueryParams()` uses `locationService.partial` |\n| `public/app/core/navigation/patch/interceptLinkClicks.ts` | Click interception uses `locationService.push` |\n| `public/app/features/alerting/unified/hooks/useURLSearchParams.ts` | Alerting params use `locationService.partial` |\n\nThese shared utilities will need to be migrated last, since they're consumed across many features.\n\n### Key Files\n\n| File | Role |\n|------|------|\n| `public/app/routes/RoutesWrapper.tsx` | Router setup, compat bridge, history wiring |\n| `public/app/AppWrapper.tsx` | Renders `<Routes>` / `<Route>` elements |\n| `public/app/routes/routes.tsx` | Central route definitions |\n| `public/app/core/navigation/GrafanaRoute.tsx` | Per-route wrapper (auth, RBAC, error boundary) |\n| `packages/grafana-runtime/src/services/LocationService.tsx` | `locationService` implementation |\n| `.cursor/skills/react-router-migration/SKILL.md` | Migration workflow documentation |\n| `.cursor/agents/react-router-migration-status-explorer.md` | Status exploration agent |\n\n### Dashboard-Scene Routing\n\nThe dashboard system has its own routing concern: a `DashboardPageProxy` component decides whether to render the **legacy dashboard** or the **new dashboard-scene** implementation based on feature flags (`dashboardScene`, `dashboardNewLayouts`, `dashboardSceneForViewers`). This is separate from but related to the React Router migration — the scene-based dashboard system uses `@grafana/scenes` which has its own URL state management patterns.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nGrafana's routing is in a transitional state: the app runs React Router v5 with a v6 compatibility layer, and the team is incrementally migrating ~150+ files from the imperative `locationService` singleton to React Router's `useNavigate` hook. The migration is feature-flagged per area, with provisioning fully done and many other areas still pending. The ultimate goal is to remove the `locationService` dependency entirely and run on pure React Router v6.",
